{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793510831,"nonce":"BuXcnv8251S2a14+hef5kSuA3ww=","title":"Lua GC: a dead spill slot in a resume function keeps an object alive, and gc.lua's GC1 loops forever on arm64","message":"official_suite/basic_all (zylua -e _U=true all.lua, default ladder, release) runs into its 600 s limit on the Mac (arm64); basic_all_llvm stops in 16 s and the NUC (x86_64) finishes both. all.lua reaches gc.lua's ephemeron section, and its first GC() never returns: tracegc prints a dot per collection without end.\n\nReproducer: gc.lua's GC1 (lines 57 to 74) alone, with a print in each finalizer. Loops 1 and 2 finish; loop 3 (`repeat local i; u = function () return i end until finish`) never sees its finalizer run. Each loop alone finishes, and the whole function finishes with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR=1. A binary from 2026-10-31 (before 1cb0f74c) behaves the same.\n\nCause, from ZYNTAX_GC_EXPLAIN (with the survivor report widened to automatic collections) plus ZYNTAX_DUMP_VCODE=GC1: the finalizable object is reached from `[sp, #8]` in the frame of lua$GC1$1$resume2, the resume function entered at loop 2's header, which runs the rest of GC1 including loop 3. Before loop 3 it spills the fresh table that setmetatable is given to `[sp, #8]` around the call; nothing in loop 3 writes that slot again, so the conservative stack scan reads the dead word in every collection. x86_64's register assignment happens not to leave it.\n\nThis is the stale-word class 558e28e lists (the stack scan is conservative), here as a hang. The interpreter clears its own dead frames (13acfd62), but a live Cranelift frame's dead spill slots are out of reach. A fix needs JIT frames scanned precisely: Cranelift user stack maps for the values that hold objects, and a frame walk that reads only a JIT frame's live slots at its call site.","files":null}]}